/**
\page api_overview C++ API overview

The computational engine underlying the <span class=SC>scuff-em</span>
command-line applications is a set of C++ libraries.  If the standalone
programs are not flexible enough for your needs, you can write your own
C++ (or python) codes that link against these libraries directly; the
[core-library reference](@ref md_doc_2docs_2_a_p_i_2libscuff) walks
through this process with fully worked examples.

\section api_libraries Libraries

<table>
<tr><th>Library<th>Header<th>Description
<tr><td>\b libscuff
    <td>\c libscuff.h
    <td>The core library.  The central class is
        \ref scuff::RWGGeometry "RWGGeometry," which stores all
        necessary data on a scattering geometry described by a
        <tt>.scuffgeo</tt> file and provides methods for assembling and
        solving BEM systems and post-processing the solutions.
        Surfaces are described by \ref scuff::RWGSurface "RWGSurface."
<tr><td>\b libscuffSolver
    <td>\c libscuffSolver.h
    <td>A higher-level convenience layer, \ref scuff::scuffSolver
        "scuffSolver," that caches the assembled BEM matrix to accelerate
        computations at multiple frequencies, incident fields, and
        post-processing evaluations on one and the same geometry.
<tr><td>\b libStaticSolver
    <td>\c libStaticSolver.h
    <td>\ref scuff::StaticSolver "StaticSolver," the quasistatic
        (pure-electrostatics) counterpart of RWGGeometry.
<tr><td>\b libIncField
    <td>\c libIncField.h
    <td>The \ref IncField "IncField" class hierarchy describes incident
        fields: plane waves, point sources, gaussian beams, and
        user-specified field profiles.
<tr><td>\b libhmat
    <td>\c libhmat.h
    <td>Simple row-major dense matrix/vector classes (\ref HMatrix,
        \ref HVector) used throughout <span class=SC>scuff-em</span>,
        with helpers for linear algebra (wrappers around LAPACK for
        \ref Eig "eigendecomposition," SVD, QR, ...).
<tr><td>\b libhrutil
    <td>\c libhrutil.h
    <td>Miscellaneous utility routines: geometry helpers, cache
        management, timing, logging, command-line conveniences
        (\c GetCommandLineOptions), file I/O helpers.
<tr><td>\b libMatProp
    <td>\c libMatProp.h
    <td>The \ref MatProp "MatProp" class describes frequency-dependent
        material permittivities/permeabilities, including tabulated
        materials, Drude/Lorentz models, and interpolated material
        files.
<tr><td>\b libSubstrate
    <td>\c libSubstrate.h
    <td>\ref Substrate "Substrate" describes layered dielectric
        substrates and computes their Green's functions.
<tr><td>\b libSpherical
    <td>\c libSpherical.h
    <td>Vector spherical waves and spherical-basis helpers.
</table>

\section api_conventions Conventions

- All library entities live in the \c scuff namespace; source files
  typically \c \#include \c <libscuff.h> and issue
  <tt>using namespace scuff;</tt>.
- Matrix and vector objects are \ref HMatrix "HMatrix" /
  \ref HVector "HVector" instances.  Modern <span class=SC>scuff-em</span>
  API methods follow a factory/fill convention: an allocating overload
  returns a \c std::unique_ptr to a freshly allocated object, while a
  filling overload stores into a caller-owned object of exactly the
  right dimensions (wrong dimensions are a fatal error).
- Geometries are created with \c new and managed by pointer; copying
  is neither used nor safe.

\section api_examples Example programs

The \c examples directory of the source distribution contains
fully worked example programs that access the core library directly;
see \ref md_doc_2docs_2examples_2examples "the examples overview" and
the individual tutorial pages linked from the documentation home page.

*/
